  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The synthesized evidence is predominantly correlational and heavily concentrated in large cities within high-income nations, limiting its immediate applicability to other contexts.",
      "what": "This scoping review synthesizes 71 journal articles published between 2015 and 2025 to map artificial intelligence applications in urban mobility and their environmental impacts. The analysis uses thematic coding and causal loop diagrams to categorize machine learning and deep learning techniques across transport modes, focusing on demand prediction, traffic optimization, and emissions monitoring.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that while AI-driven traffic and fleet optimization improve energy efficiency, substantial emissions reductions are not achieved by technology alone and require a structural modal shift toward public transit and active transportation."
    }
